  "result": "At the edge of a small northern town, there is a lighthouse that no longer guides ships. Its lamp was replaced by satellites, radar, and glowing screens in wheelhouses, yet every evening someone climbs the narrow iron stairs and switches it on.\n\nThe keeper is not officially a keeper. She runs the bakery beside the harbor, wakes before dawn, and knows which fishermen prefer rye bread to sweet rolls. At sunset, after the last tray is washed, she takes a ring of old keys from a hook shaped like an anchor. Tourists assume the ritual is for them. They photograph the beam sweeping over black water and say it makes the town feel romantic.\n\nBut the light is for the people ashore.\n\nIt passes over kitchen windows, bus stops, boats tied safely to pilings, and the weathered faces of those standing quietly at the seawall. For a few seconds each minute, every object becomes clear: the gulls\u2019 white wings, a child\u2019s red scarf, the silver seams of rain on the road. Then darkness returns, softened by the certainty that brightness will come around again.\n\nNo one has made the task mandatory. The town council has discussed closing the lighthouse, citing maintenance costs and modern alternatives. Each time, the meeting room fills with residents carrying loaves, nets, school drawings, and stories. They do not argue that the lighthouse is necessary. They argue that it is faithful.\n\nPerhaps that is what many old customs are: not efficient answers, but promises becoming horizon.",
